== Ad Processionem | The Procession
I: Ex Processionario O.P. (1913), pp. 24–33
 = From the Processionarium O.P. (1913), pp. 24–33
R: ^CANTORIBUS inchoantibus Antiphonam *Cum appropinquáret.* Processio egrediatur hoc ordine : Primo præcedat Frater aliquis Clericus in superpelliceo, vel aliquis Conversus, si pauci sint Fratres, cum Aqua benedicta, quam procedens aspergat; post eum Ceroferarii; deinde Cruciferarius cum Cruce nudata; postea reliqui Fratres ordine suo, junioribus præcedentibus; ultimo Prior cum Diacono a dextris, aliquas Reliquias deferente, et Subdiacono a sinistris, portante libellum processionalem.
 = As the Cantors begin the Antiphon *Cum appropinquáret,* the Procession goes out in this order: first goes a cleric brother in surplice, or a lay brother if the brethren are few, with holy water, which he sprinkles as he goes; after him the candle-bearers; then the cross-bearer with the Cross uncovered; then the other brethren in their order, the juniors first; last the Prior with the Deacon on his right, carrying some relics, and the Subdeacon on his left, carrying the processional book.
T: {Ant. toni vij.} Cum appropinquáret Dóminus Jerosólymam, misit duos ex discípulis suis, dicens : Ite in castéllum quod est contra vos, et inveniétis pullum ásinæ alligátum, super quem nullus hóminum sedit : sólvite, et addúcite mihi : Si quis vos interrogáverit, dícite : Opus Dómino est. Solvéntes adduxérunt ad Jesum, et imposuérunt illi vestiménta, et super eum sedit. Alii expandébant vestiménta sua in via : Alii ramos de arbóribus sternébant; et qui sequebántur clamábant : Hosánna, benedíctus qui venit in nómine Dómini; benedíctum regnum Patris nostri David : Hosánna in excélsis : miserére nobis, Fili David.
 = {Ant. mode 7} When the Lord drew near to Jerusalem, He sent two of His disciples, saying: Go into the village that is over against you, and you will find the colt of an ass tied, on which no man has sat; loose it and bring it to Me. If anyone asks you, say: The Lord has need of it. Loosing it, they brought it to Jesus, and laid their garments upon it, and He sat upon it. Some spread their garments in the way; others strewed branches from the trees; and those who followed cried out: Hosanna, blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord; blessed is the kingdom of our father David. Hosanna in the highest: have mercy on us, Son of David.
G: op13-cumappropinquaret
R: Dum prædicta Antiphona cantatur, fiat prima Statio in prima parte Claustri. In unaquaque autem Statione Cruciferarius et Ceroferarii vultus suos et imaginem Crucifixi versos habeant ad Conventum, nisi forte fieret Statio ante exteriorem introitum Ecclesiæ, vel Chori : tunc enim prædicti Ministri vertere debent vultus ad Altare, imagine tamen Crucifixi versa ad Conventum. Prior autem stans ultimo cum Diacono et Subdiacono, ut dictum est, vultus suos habeant versos ad Crucem; ceteri Fratres stent semper versis vultibus ad se invicem. Deferens vero Aquam benedictam, deposita Aqua ante Crucem, collocet se ultimo loco in Choro suo, si est Clericus; vel stet aliquantulum retro Crucem, si est Conversus. Finita Antiphona præcedenti, Cantoribus inchoantibus Antiphonam *Collegérunt,* procedatur ad secundam Stationem, quæ fiat in tertia parte Claustri.
 = While this Antiphon is sung, the first Station is made in the first part of the cloister. At each Station the cross-bearer and candle-bearers keep their faces and the image of the Crucified turned towards the Community, unless the Station is made before the outer entrance of the church or of the choir; for then these ministers must turn their faces towards the altar, the image of the Crucified, however, being turned towards the Community. The Prior, standing last with the Deacon and Subdeacon, as has been said, keeps his face turned towards the Cross; the other brethren always stand facing one another. The one carrying the holy water, having set the water down before the Cross, takes the last place in his choir if he is a cleric, or stands a little behind the Cross if he is a lay brother. When the preceding Antiphon is finished, the Cantors beginning the Antiphon *Collegérunt,* they go on to the second Station, which is made in the third part of the cloister.
T: {Ant. toni ij.} Collegérunt Pontífices et Pharisǽi concílium, et dicébant : * Quid fácimus? Quia hic homo multa signa facit : si dimíttimus eum sic, omnes credent in eum; et forte vénient Románi, et tollent nostrum locum, et gentem.
 = {Ant. mode 2} The chief priests and the Pharisees gathered a council, and said: * What are we doing? For this man works many signs; if we let him alone so, all will believe in him, and perhaps the Romans will come and take away our place and nation.
G: op13-collegerunt
R: Finita prædicta Antiphona, dum fit secunda Statio, duo Fratres in medio Processionis stantes ante Crucem cantent :
 = When this Antiphon is finished, while the second Station is made, two brethren standing in the middle of the Procession before the Cross sing:
T: {℣.} Unus autem ex ipsis, Cáiphas nómine, cum esset Póntifex anni illíus, prophetávit, dicens : Expedit vobis, ut unus moriátur homo pro pópulo, et non tota gens péreat. Ab illo ergo die cogitavérunt interfícere eum, dicéntes : * Quid fácimus.
 = {℣.} But one of them, named Caiphas, being the high priest that year, prophesied, saying: It is expedient for you that one man should die for the people, and that the whole nation perish not. From that day therefore they devised to put Him to death, saying: * What are we doing.
G: op13-unusautem
R: Cum autem inchoabitur post {℣.} resumptio *Quid fácimus,* procedatur ad tertiam Stationem, quæ fiat ante introitum Ecclesiæ anteriorem, vel in Ecclesia laicorum ante introitum Chori. Finita autem resumptione prædicta, Cantores genua flectendo incipiant Antiphonam *Ave Rex noster.* In cujus inceptione Fratres omnes (præter Priorem cum Ministris, et Cruciferarium, et Ceroferarios,) versis vultibus ad Crucem genua flectentes Antiphonam prosequantur. Cum autem dixerint *Redémptor mundi,* omnes se erigant, stantes versi ad Crucem usque ad finem Antiphonæ.
 = When, after the {℣.}, the repetition *Quid fácimus* is begun, they go on to the third Station, which is made before the front entrance of the church, or in the lay church before the entrance of the choir. When this repetition is finished, the Cantors, kneeling, begin the Antiphon *Ave Rex noster.* At its beginning all the brethren (except the Prior with the ministers, the cross-bearer and the candle-bearers), turning towards the Cross, kneel and continue the Antiphon. When they have said *Redémptor mundi,* all rise and stand facing the Cross until the end of the Antiphon.
T: {Omnes genua flectant. Ant. toni ij.} Ave Rex noster, Fili David, Redémptor mundi, {Omnes surgant.} quem Prophétæ prædixérunt Salvatórem dómui Israël esse ventúrum : te enim ad salutárem víctimam Pater misit in mundum, quem exspectábant omnes Sancti ab orígine mundi. Et nunc Hosánna Fílio David : benedíctus qui venit in nómine Dómini : Hosánna in excélsis.
 = {All kneel. Ant. mode 2} Hail, our King, Son of David, Redeemer of the world, {All rise.} whom the prophets foretold as the Saviour who was to come to the house of Israel; for the Father sent You into the world as the saving victim, You whom all the Saints awaited from the beginning of the world. And now Hosanna to the Son of David: blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord: Hosanna in the highest.
G: op13-averex
R: Dum autem prædicta Antiphona cantatur, duo Fratres, quibus Cantor injunxerit, intrent Ecclesiam, vel Chorum, et clauso ostio stantes versis vultibus ad Conventum, finita Antiphona prædicta, cantent totum sequentem Versum : *Glória, laus.* Et dum hic Versus cantatur ab ipsis duobus Fratribus, Conventus vertat se ad Crucem.
 = While this Antiphon is sung, two brethren appointed by the Cantor go into the church or the choir and, the door being shut, stand facing the Community; when the Antiphon is finished, they sing the whole of the following verse, *Glória, laus.* And while this verse is sung by the two brethren, the Community turns towards the Cross.
T: {Hymnus. toni j.} Glória, laus et honor tibi sit, Rex Christe, Redémptor, cui pueríle decus prompsit Hosánna pium. {Chorus.} Glória.
 = {Hymn. mode 1} Glory, praise and honour be to You, Christ, King and Redeemer, to whom the glory of children sang a loving Hosanna. {Choir.} Glory.
G: op13-glorialaus
R: Quo finito, Chorus eumdem {℣.} totum repetat. Deinde dicti duo Fratres cantent sequentes Versus, et Chorus post unumquemque respondeat : *Glória, laus.*
 = When it is finished, the Choir repeats the whole of the same {℣.} Then the two brethren sing the following verses, and after each the Choir answers: *Glória, laus.*
T: {Duo Fratres ℣.} Israël es tu Rex, Davídis et ínclyta proles, nómine qui in Dómini, Rex benedícte, venis. {Chorus.} Glória.
 = {Two brethren ℣.} You are the King of Israel, David's glorious offspring, the blessed King who comes in the name of the Lord. {Choir.} Glory.
T: {℣.} Cœtus in excélsis te laudat cǽlicus omnis, et mortális homo, et cuncta creáta simul. {Chorus.} Glória.
 = {℣.} The whole company on high praises You, and mortal man, and all creation together. {Choir.} Glory.
T: {℣.} Plebs hebrǽa tibi cum palmis óbvia venit; cum prece, voto, hymnis, ádsumus ecce tibi. {Chorus.} Glória.
 = {℣.} The Hebrew people came to meet You with palms; behold, we are here before You with prayer, vow and hymns. {Choir.} Glory.
R: Cantato ultimo Versu, prædicti duo Fratres ostium aperiant, et ad Processionem in locis suis revertantur. Repetito a Choro *Glória, laus,* post ultimum {℣.} ad introitum Ecclesiæ vel Chori, incipiatur a Priore, Cantore ei deferente, {℟.} *Ingrediénte.* Et Conventu ipsum prosequente, Processio Chorum ingrediatur.
 = When the last verse has been sung, the two brethren open the door and return to their places in the Procession. When the Choir has repeated *Glória, laus* after the last {℣.}, at the entrance of the church or choir the Prior, the Cantor bringing it to him, begins the {℟.} *Ingrediénte;* and as the Community continues it, the Procession enters the choir.
T: {℟. toni ij.} Ingrediénte Dómino in sanctam civitátem, Hebræórum púeri resurrectiónem vitæ pronuntiántes, * Cum ramis palmárum Hosánna clamábant in excélsis. {℣.} Cumque audíssent quia venit Jesus Jerosólymam, exiérunt óbviam ei. * Cum ramis.
 = {℟. mode 2} As the Lord entered the holy city, the children of the Hebrews, proclaiming the resurrection of life, * with branches of palms cried out: Hosanna in the highest. {℣.} And when they had heard that Jesus was coming to Jerusalem, they went out to meet Him. * With branches.
G: op13-ingrediente
R: Cum Conventus Chorum intrat, singuli vadant ad sedes suas : et qui Aquam portavit, ad Sacristiam revertatur. Prior vero et Ministri se ordinent, sicut ad aspersionem Aquæ benedictæ consueverunt, Cruciferario tenente Crucem ante gradus Presbyterii. Finito {℟.} *Ingrediénte,* duo Fratres cantent {℣.} *Cumque audíssent.* Finita post {℣.} resumptione *Cum ramis,* Ceroferarii, Cantore eis deferente, dicant :
 = When the Community enters the choir, each goes to his seat, and the one who carried the water returns to the sacristy. The Prior and ministers place themselves as they usually do for the sprinkling of holy water, the cross-bearer holding the Cross before the steps of the presbytery. When the {℟.} *Ingrediénte* is finished, two brethren sing the {℣.} *Cumque audíssent.* When the repetition *Cum ramis* after the {℣.} is finished, the candle-bearers, the Cantor bringing it to them, say:
T: {℣.} De ore leónis líbera me, Dómine. {℟.} Et a córnibus unicórnium humilitátem meam.
 = {℣.} Deliver me, O Lord, from the lion's mouth. {℟.} And my lowliness from the horns of the unicorns.
G: op13-deoreleonis
R: Deinde Prior absque *Dóminus vobíscum,* præmisso *Orémus,* dicat Orationem eo modo quo dicitur ad Horas :
 = Then the Prior, without *Dóminus vobíscum,* *Orémus* being said first, says the Oration in the manner in which it is said at the Hours:
T: Orémus.
 = Let us pray.
T: ^OMNIPOTENS sempitérne Deus, qui humáno géneri, ad imitándum humilitátis exémplum, Salvatórem nostrum carnem súmere, et Crucem subíre fecísti : concéde propítius : ut et patiéntiæ ipsíus habére documénta, et Resurrectiónis consórtia mereámur : Per eúmdem Christum Dóminum nostrum. {℟.} Amen.
 = Almighty and everlasting God, who would have our Saviour become man and undergo the Cross, to give mankind an example of humility to imitate, mercifully grant that we may both learn the lessons of His patience and share in His resurrection: through the same Christ our Lord. {℟.} Amen.
R: Dum Oratio dicitur, Cruciferarius Crucem reponat in loco suo ad cornu Evangelii, quæ discooperta usque ad Completorium remaneat. Oratione finita, Prior et Ministri, ad Sacristiam redeant.
 = While the Oration is said, the cross-bearer puts the Cross back in its place at the Gospel corner, where it remains uncovered until Compline. When the Oration is finished, the Prior and ministers go back to the sacristy.
